WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.640
- I think we don't talk enough

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:04.100
about how no and failures

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.896
are the foundations for yes and wins.

00:00:06.896 --> 00:00:07.729
(upbeat music)

00:00:07.729 --> 00:00:08.562
(yells)

00:00:08.562 --> 00:00:10.667
You got your perspective.

00:00:10.667 --> 00:00:13.650
(audience cheers)

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:14.483
I just wanna be happy.

00:00:14.483 --> 00:00:15.553
Don't you wanna be happy?

00:00:16.647 --> 00:00:20.047
(upbeat music)

00:00:20.047 --> 00:00:23.047
(audience applauds)

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:27.510
If you're like me and you're over 40,

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:30.300
you actually remember
the world pre-internet.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.460
Which for a lot of people
in here doesn't exist,

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.823
and so from afar,

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:39.830
he hates when I pontificate about this,

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:42.940
but when you are literally the leader

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:46.620
the rain maker of Hollywood,

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:49.430
during a pre-internet world,

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:52.430
you are not only at the top of Hollywood,

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:55.022
you know you're talking
to somebody that thinks

00:00:55.022 --> 00:00:56.510
that Hollywood has been the great engine

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:58.890
that has built the American brand

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:00.410
over the last hundred years.

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:02.750
So if you think about having a decade

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:06.920
when nothing significant
doesn't run through you

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:08.780
or your organization,

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:13.220
there is no current comp in our society

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:15.250
of like dictating culture

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:18.100
because the internet has amortized it out

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.520
to a lot of leaders.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.630
And so when you think about

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:25.830
four to five or six or
seven individuals' map

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:28.350
to the level of, it's not a money thing,

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:32.470
it's an impact on culture and
really just a level of power,

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:34.850
we just, there aren't a lot of individuals

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:39.850
who've had a run on dictating
what this country consumed

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:42.600
and thus the world more
than what Michael did

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:44.460
at the top of CAA.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:45.440
- What did you learn from him,

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:48.033
observing him the last 20 years?

00:01:49.224 --> 00:01:51.620
- Being obnoxiously powerful is cool.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:54.204
I'm kidding, I'm kidding, I'm kidding.

00:01:54.204 --> 00:01:57.110
I think what, I think it's a
little bit different, actually.

00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:58.623
I have a very nuanced answer.

00:01:59.481 --> 00:02:02.800
That is definitely not what I learned,

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:04.665
and not something I think a lot about.

00:02:04.665 --> 00:02:05.978
What I did learn, though,

00:02:05.978 --> 00:02:06.890
or more importantly,

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:08.480
as I started digging into Michael's career

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:11.863
as my career changed, and you would just,

00:02:11.863 --> 00:02:14.090
cause I didn't really grow up
with a eye towards Hollywood,

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:17.203
A, all my buddies, and pals and friends

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:21.319
that looked toward L.A. and Hollywood

00:02:21.319 --> 00:02:25.000
of a certain age, this
is like way up there.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:28.150
Number two, my whole career, right now,

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:30.730
I'm currently building
a company that's goal

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:33.970
is to buy IP and businesses in the future,

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:38.280
and repackage them and merchandise them.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.610
I think a lot of entrepreneurs here,

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:43.510
and not only entrepreneurs, non-profits,

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:45.590
politicians, taste makers, whatever

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:47.810
your goals and ambitions are, a lot of you

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:50.000
are missing, everything's right,

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.405
except you didn't figure out how to create

00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:53.665
the proper container for it,

00:02:53.665 --> 00:02:56.950
and how to merchandise that container.

00:02:56.950 --> 00:03:01.743
What Michael spearheaded
was a packaging of talent,

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:06.750
the IP not the distribution
that gave his clients

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:09.810
and his organization the leverage,

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:13.600
and was able to extract
out dollars from the system

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:15.500
that they were entitled to that they

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:16.680
didn't get in the past,

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:19.030
no different than a players' union did

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:20.800
for athletes in sports.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.250
So, what did I learn?

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:25.800
That it reinforced my
intuition that packaging

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.420
and merchandising is as important

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:30.740
as having the chops for it.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:33.410
- Can you continue on this,
Michael, on packaging.

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:35.710
That really is something you're known for.

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:37.720
- So, it's important to bear in mind,

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:39.880
you have to, in the course of this hour

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:42.810
or 45 minutes we're spending together,

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:44.820
one has to start thinking of time frames.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:47.023
Gary mentioned it articulately.

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:51.770
Pre-internet, post internet, a completely

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:55.550
different approach to distribution.

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:58.170
Today, if Elliot had a
good piece of content,

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:01.620
he wanted to put up, as Gary does also,

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:03.520
they can create audiences.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:06.030
They can create small, niche audiences.

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:08.210
They can create large audiences

00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:11.450
that cut across a large demographic.

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:13.210
When I worked in the
entertainment business

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:15.800
from the 60's, yes, the 60's,

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:19.790
all the way through early
2000, when the business

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:22.260
started to change because of the internet,

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:26.800
there was a very large barrier to entry.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:30.330
So, if any of you out there had an idea,

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:33.710
there were very limited ways
to get it to the public,

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:35.470
very limited ways.

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:37.140
Actually, to be very specific,

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:40.770
you had CBS, NBC, ABC, later on Fox.

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:43.460
You had seven movie studios,
five publishing houses,

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:46.010
five record companies, and probably 10

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:47.170
different companies in Europe.

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:48.430
That was it.

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:52.130
What we tried to do is move the leverage,

00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:55.140
as Gary mentioned, from the buy side,

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:58.030
who were buying your
ideas, to the sell side.

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:00.870
We did it by packaging ideas.

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:04.280
So, if Gary had an idea
for, in those days a film,

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:08.530
or TV show, a book, a
record, a magazine article,

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:12.430
or today a podcast, we would proceed

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:15.370
to try to add as many elements
around that as possible

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:19.290
before going to a buyer so
that the more elements we had,

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:23.340
the buyer only had two choices, yes or no.

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:24.880
Then we would back it up.

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:26.690
Today, it's a different game.

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:28.420
Although, I will say you can still

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:31.180
play the game we played in today's market.

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:32.730
You've done a good job of that.

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:37.160
- Yeah, I mean, so much
of this is about leverage.

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:39.360
It's kind of how I think about kindness,

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:40.490
in the biggest macro.

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:43.400
I literally think kindness
is the ultimate leverage

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:44.710
in perpetuity.

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:46.470
So, something as frothy as that,

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:50.370
or building community or
being an execution player.

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:51.960
It's all about the leverage.

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:55.230
If you're not commoditized,
you got a shot.

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:57.490
- But, with leverage comes the connotation

00:05:57.490 --> 00:05:59.490
of non-kindness, by the way.

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:01.580
Because you have that leverage,

00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:02.920
it's a question of how you use it.

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:05.750
I would, Gary and I were
talking before the session

00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:09.560
with Elliot, and I will say
that in today's marketplace,

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:12.280
many of the things that worked in the past

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:14.610
probably would have to be adjusted,

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:17.160
and either ramped up in certain areas,

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:19.230
or ramped way down in others.

00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:21.785
Leverage is a very dangerous thing

00:06:21.785 --> 00:06:24.670
in an internet world where
everything's instantaneous,

00:06:24.670 --> 00:06:28.223
and everything is communicated
if it's not used properly.

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:31.343
- How did you package Rain Man?

00:06:32.205 --> 00:06:34.010
And, how did you break
through those walls?

00:06:34.010 --> 00:06:36.250
- So, Rain Man started as a screen play

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:38.910
that was turned down by Warner Brothers.

00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:40.550
I'll try to give you
the short story of this

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:42.630
cause it actually took three years

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:44.963
just to get it off the ground.

00:06:47.353 --> 00:06:48.830
The studio didn't wanna do it,

00:06:48.830 --> 00:06:52.530
which was really raw meat for CAA.

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:55.070
When a studio didn't wanna do something,

00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:57.410
we wanted to show that we could do it

00:06:57.410 --> 00:07:01.000
as being really a company
that was patterned

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:04.466
a lot after what MGM was in the 40's,

00:07:04.466 --> 00:07:08.830
30's, 40's, and 50's, which
was a repository of talent.

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:12.580
So, anybody that had any
kind of talent at all

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:16.010
was somebody that MGM tried to acquire,

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:17.370
buy, put under contract.

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:19.780
We tried to do the same thing.

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:22.420
With Rain Man, we had a
screen play turned down

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:24.620
with nothing attached to it.

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:26.520
We immediately, after reading the script,

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:28.986
gave it to Dustin
Hoffman, who fell in love

00:07:28.986 --> 00:07:32.570
with both roles, but
particularly the role,

00:07:32.570 --> 00:07:34.820
the lead role of Raymond.

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:38.070
We then decided to try
to cast the other role

00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:40.180
prior to going to a director.

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:42.890
We came up with the idea of Tom Cruise.

00:07:42.890 --> 00:07:44.730
It sounds great after the fact,

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:47.540
but when we did it, there
was a front page article

00:07:47.540 --> 00:07:50.700
in the Los Angeles Times
saying that we were idiots,

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:53.030
that there was no way that those two guys

00:07:53.030 --> 00:07:53.910
could be brothers.

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:55.801
They didn't look alike.

00:07:55.801 --> 00:07:57.222
They had nothing to do with each other.

00:07:57.222 --> 00:08:01.390
Hoffman had gone to UCLA to meet

00:08:01.390 --> 00:08:05.630
with the head of the doctor
who ran the autism program,

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:07.890
and discovered that in autistic families,

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:10.790
the kids don't necessarily look the same.

00:08:10.790 --> 00:08:15.770
It's just, it's kind of
a strange part of nature.

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:17.490
They can or they don't have to.

00:08:17.490 --> 00:08:20.640
In this case, the majority
of the time, they don't.

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:23.580
So, casting Cruise was very important

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:25.710
cause we wanted that demographic,

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:28.670
and Hoffman had his own demographic.

00:08:28.670 --> 00:08:31.010
Long story short, we had a director,

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:33.840
and the director couldn't figure out

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:37.720
how to get the picture made quickly,

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:40.390
so we went to a second director.

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:43.140
The first director was Marty Brest,

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:45.770
who had done Beverly Hills Cop.

00:08:45.770 --> 00:08:46.810
He couldn't figure it out.

00:08:46.810 --> 00:08:50.930
We went to Barry Levinson, who ultimately

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:52.140
directed the picture.

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:55.180
He got called away to do
Good Morning, Vietnam.

00:08:55.180 --> 00:08:58.100
We then went to Steven Spielberg,

00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:00.080
who had trouble with the script,

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:02.500
and he got called away
to do something else.

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:04.520
We then went to Sydney Pollack,

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:07.490
who couldn't figure the piece out

00:09:07.490 --> 00:09:09.920
cause it didn't have a third act.

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:12.510
Then, by that time, it had no third act

00:09:12.510 --> 00:09:15.100
because an autistic person, unfortunately,

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:16.520
doesn't get better.

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:18.610
Sydney had a very linear mind.

00:09:18.610 --> 00:09:21.380
So, he wanted to have an ending.

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:23.810
Anyways, in conclusion, by that time,

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:25.820
believe it or not, Good
Morning, Vietnam was done,

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:27.283
and we released it.

00:09:28.244 --> 00:09:29.337
Levinson was available, and I called him

00:09:29.337 --> 00:09:31.860
and said you should
really come back to this.

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:33.960
He went back, read the script,

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:35.290
and he said there is no third act.

00:09:35.290 --> 00:09:37.030
This guy doesn't get better.

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:38.980
I'm gonna make a movie that I'm gonna term

00:09:38.980 --> 00:09:40.363
two schmucks in a car.

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:44.222
That was the working title of the movie,

00:09:44.222 --> 00:09:47.070
Two Schmucks in a Car.

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:49.520
He made a road movie,
which for those of you

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:52.830
that are a little older,
and I don't see many of you,

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:54.810
there are all these
movies made in the 40's

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:56.930
with Bob Hope, and Bing Crosby,

00:09:56.930 --> 00:09:59.660
and old time movie stars
called road movies.

00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:01.800
So, Levinson did what Picasso did.

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:04.920
He appropriated somebody else's idea,

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:06.980
which is highly acceptable, by the way,

00:10:06.980 --> 00:10:09.450
as long as you don't infringe on their IP,

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:11.420
and he made a road movie.

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:14.020
Low and behold, the movie came out,

00:10:14.020 --> 00:10:17.263
and it was a dismal
failure opening weekend.

00:10:17.263 --> 00:10:18.266
- [Gary] Is that right?

00:10:18.266 --> 00:10:20.580
- Yeah, it did $6 million for a weekend,

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:22.790
and the New York Times wrote an article

00:10:22.790 --> 00:10:25.140
that Cruise, Hoffman, and Levinson

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:27.808
were gonna fire me, and that the head

00:10:27.808 --> 00:10:32.010
of the studio that did the
actual, physical distribution

00:10:32.010 --> 00:10:33.660
was gonna get fired.

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:36.500
What they didn't figure out
was a word of mouth movie,

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:40.730
and the movie did $6 million
for the next 50 weeks,

00:10:40.730 --> 00:10:42.710
the next 50 weeks.

00:10:42.710 --> 00:10:45.650
Then it eventually got
over $1 billion of gross,

00:10:45.650 --> 00:10:48.430
and in that timeframe in
the 80's was important.

00:10:48.430 --> 00:10:50.362
It won six Academy Awards.

00:10:50.362 --> 00:10:53.507
(audience applauds)

00:10:53.507 --> 00:10:54.340
Thank you.

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:58.440
I only go on about this,
and then I'll shut up,

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:01.610
but it's really something
to take to heart.

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:04.470
It's called don't ever give up
if you believe in something.

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:06.289
Just don't give up.

00:11:06.289 --> 00:11:08.910
(audience applauds)

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:10.200
We were told don't do it.

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:11.650
We were told we'd get fired.

00:11:11.650 --> 00:11:14.430
We were told that by the first director

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:15.490
the movie was unmakeable.

00:11:15.490 --> 00:11:17.720
We were told by the second director,

00:11:17.720 --> 00:11:18.553
it wasn't makeable.

00:11:18.553 --> 00:11:20.526
The third director, same
thing, fourth director.

00:11:20.526 --> 00:11:22.860
The movie got made, and it was a huge hit.

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:25.347
So, when you're into something out there,

00:11:25.347 --> 00:11:27.410
whether it's an idea, or
a deal you're working on,

00:11:27.410 --> 00:11:30.150
or something where you know your stomach

00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:34.340
and your head have met each
other, just don't stop.

00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:35.970
- [Elliot] Gary--

00:11:35.970 --> 00:11:36.810
- Real quick, Elliot, I apologize.

00:11:36.810 --> 00:11:41.810
Michael, when your stomach
and head have connected

00:11:42.170 --> 00:11:45.040
on something not named Rain Man,

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:49.130
give us something that
has failed miserably

00:11:49.130 --> 00:11:51.927
that your stomach and head connected,

00:11:51.927 --> 00:11:53.180
you were sure, you saw it through,

00:11:53.180 --> 00:11:55.540
you put your name on
it, and it shit the bed

00:11:55.540 --> 00:11:56.973
at the theater.

00:11:56.973 --> 00:11:59.000
(audience laughs)

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:00.150
- God, I'm embarrassed.

00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:05.150
One that I worked on
with one of my partners,

00:12:05.150 --> 00:12:08.790
probably one of the biggest disasters

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:11.700
we ever did was Rhinestone
with Sylvester Stallone

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:13.386
and Dolly Parton.

00:12:13.386 --> 00:12:15.530
(audience laughs)

00:12:15.530 --> 00:12:17.460
It looked really good, Gary, on paper.

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:19.270
- [Gary] I get it, man.

00:12:19.270 --> 00:12:21.300
- It kinda reminds me of a football play.

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:22.650
For those of you who like football,

00:12:22.650 --> 00:12:25.550
every play is designed to be perfect,

00:12:25.550 --> 00:12:26.990
and it's a touchdown.

00:12:26.990 --> 00:12:28.710
If that's the case, football scores

00:12:28.710 --> 00:12:31.771
would be 270 to 220.

00:12:31.771 --> 00:12:32.769
- [Gary] That's right.

00:12:32.769 --> 00:12:34.760
- Instead of 20 to 15.

00:12:34.760 --> 00:12:37.310
The reality is this had
everything going for it on paper.

00:12:37.310 --> 00:12:40.330
She was on fire in the music area,

00:12:40.330 --> 00:12:43.430
had five back to back hit singles.

00:12:43.430 --> 00:12:47.670
Sly had done one giant hit
action movie after the other.

00:12:47.670 --> 00:12:49.830
He was, at that time, the highest paid

00:12:49.830 --> 00:12:52.030
action star in the business.

00:12:52.030 --> 00:12:54.900
We thought we put good
creative elements around it.

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:56.900
I remember sitting in the theater

00:12:56.900 --> 00:12:59.150
after the opening reel,
and getting nauseous.

00:13:01.190 --> 00:13:03.367
So, that's one, I can give you many more.

00:13:03.367 --> 00:13:04.600
- I think it's an important
point for all of us, right.

00:13:04.600 --> 00:13:07.150
I love when my intro is like he invested

00:13:07.150 --> 00:13:10.070
in Facebook and Twitter,
Tumblr, and all this stuff,

00:13:10.070 --> 00:13:12.568
and in the back of the room, every time

00:13:12.568 --> 00:13:13.550
before I go out stage, I'm like yeah,

00:13:13.550 --> 00:13:17.060
but I thought Yobongo was gonna
be the biggest of them all.

00:13:17.060 --> 00:13:21.640
Right, I think that we don't talk enough

00:13:21.640 --> 00:13:25.180
about how no and failures
are the foundations

00:13:25.180 --> 00:13:28.310
for yes and wins, that it's a net game,

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:31.470
and too many people take
an L as a scarlet letter,

00:13:31.470 --> 00:13:34.150
which stops them from doing anything.

00:13:34.150 --> 00:13:37.040
I think that's the important
part of the equation.

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:38.450
- I think another part is, too,

00:13:38.450 --> 00:13:40.486
and I agree with that.

00:13:40.486 --> 00:13:42.371
(audience applauds)

00:13:42.371 --> 00:13:44.610
I think another part is that
don't take no for an answer.

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:47.470
I think that if you believe in something.

00:13:47.470 --> 00:13:48.830
If you believe you can do something,

00:13:48.830 --> 00:13:53.410
we came up with the thesis
that we could advertise

00:13:53.410 --> 00:13:55.709
as well as an advertiser.

00:13:55.709 --> 00:13:57.560
We thought we had the cultural roots.

00:13:57.560 --> 00:13:59.580
We had all the connectivity.

00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:01.410
We knew what the audience wanted

00:14:01.410 --> 00:14:04.160
because we were a cultural enabler,

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:05.570
basically as an agency.

00:14:05.570 --> 00:14:09.570
We weren't just guys, and
Gary just started an agency,

00:14:09.570 --> 00:14:12.600
and he understands this
now better than anyone,

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:14.910
which is you can't just
take a phone order.

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:18.330
Like, is Elliot available
to be the emcee at Summit?

00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:19.300
You can't just do that.

00:14:19.300 --> 00:14:21.620
You have to create the
job, and go to Elliot

00:14:21.620 --> 00:14:24.190
and say hey, I think you could do Summit.

00:14:24.190 --> 00:14:25.530
You'd be great at it.

00:14:25.530 --> 00:14:26.960
It'd be a great business to start.

00:14:26.960 --> 00:14:28.680
You'd be great to lead it.

00:14:28.680 --> 00:14:30.740
You have to be able to enable.

00:14:30.740 --> 00:14:33.630
People said that advertising was something

00:14:33.630 --> 00:14:35.450
that no one could do differently,

00:14:35.450 --> 00:14:38.430
and we ended up doing
all of the advertising

00:14:38.430 --> 00:14:39.670
for the Coca-Cola Company.

00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:41.013
We did it for 10 years.

00:14:42.110 --> 00:14:47.110
When we took it over, there
were 365 account executives

00:14:47.410 --> 00:14:50.560
at McCann Erickson handling a $600 billion

00:14:50.560 --> 00:14:52.070
a year account.

00:14:52.070 --> 00:14:54.830
We came up with this
concept of outsourcing,

00:14:54.830 --> 00:14:57.030
and also using our client base.

00:14:57.030 --> 00:14:59.350
We did the same exact thing they did,

00:14:59.350 --> 00:15:02.090
except they did six commercials a year.

00:15:02.090 --> 00:15:05.520
We did 35 a year for 10 years.

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:08.910
We did it with six people, six people.

00:15:08.910 --> 00:15:11.110
They had 365.

00:15:11.110 --> 00:15:13.848
Now, bear in mind, this was
the beginning of outsourcing.

00:15:13.848 --> 00:15:14.760
- Yup.

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:17.590
- So, when we did the polar
bears, which we created

00:15:17.590 --> 00:15:21.170
in house at the agency,
we had 300 animators,

00:15:21.170 --> 00:15:22.820
but not working for us.

00:15:22.820 --> 00:15:24.740
They were on somebody else's payroll.

00:15:24.740 --> 00:15:26.580
Whereas, at the other company,

00:15:26.580 --> 00:15:29.850
they were hiring all these
people, trying to manage them.

00:15:29.850 --> 00:15:30.683
It made no sense.

00:15:30.683 --> 00:15:33.600
So again, another example
about being pragmatic,

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:36.460
being confident, being
bold, and attempting

00:15:36.460 --> 00:15:40.090
to get in front of all the
criticism and negativity.

00:15:40.090 --> 00:15:42.210
- In order to win that specific account,

00:15:42.210 --> 00:15:45.000
is it true that you did
it for no commission?

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:47.340
How did that story go down?

00:15:47.340 --> 00:15:48.850
- I happened to be at a conference,

00:15:48.850 --> 00:15:50.480
a smaller version of this

00:15:50.480 --> 00:15:52.600
called the Allen and Company Conference.

00:15:52.600 --> 00:15:55.680
The CEO of Coke and the
COO of Coke were there.

00:15:55.680 --> 00:15:57.230
We were sitting, having a coffee

00:15:57.230 --> 00:15:59.960
in a very casual
environment in Sun Valley,

00:15:59.960 --> 00:16:03.480
and they were complaining that Pepsi-Cola

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:06.509
was starting to beat them on case sales.

00:16:06.509 --> 00:16:10.520
There's a book out, many years
old, called The Cola Wars.

00:16:10.520 --> 00:16:13.670
They were about as
competitive and cutthroat.

00:16:13.670 --> 00:16:15.430
You couldn't get any more cutthroat.

00:16:15.430 --> 00:16:19.260
Matter of fact, one of the subsequent CEOs

00:16:19.260 --> 00:16:22.700
of Coca-Cola was once asked
at a group meeting like this,

00:16:22.700 --> 00:16:24.820
with 1,200 people in the audience,

00:16:24.820 --> 00:16:27.390
what would he do if he was
with the CEO of Pepsi-Cola,

00:16:27.390 --> 00:16:29.630
and the CEO fell out of the boat.

00:16:29.630 --> 00:16:33.206
He said I'd shove a garden
hose down his throat.

00:16:33.206 --> 00:16:35.440
(audience laughs)

00:16:35.440 --> 00:16:36.940
Needless to say, he got fired.

00:16:38.660 --> 00:16:40.743
But, that's how competitive they were.

00:16:42.430 --> 00:16:45.470
Our goal, at the time, was
not to be in that business,

00:16:45.470 --> 00:16:49.309
but my goal was to always
listen for new ideas

00:16:49.309 --> 00:16:51.110
and new things that we could do.

00:16:51.110 --> 00:16:53.930
Every day when I came in,
and Gary knows this feeling,

00:16:53.930 --> 00:16:56.670
you walk in and you have
1,000 people working for you,

00:16:56.670 --> 00:16:59.870
when he walks into his media company,

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:03.130
he's gotta start generating ideas himself.

00:17:03.130 --> 00:17:07.580
He's gotta be generating ideas
in people that work for him.

00:17:07.580 --> 00:17:09.410
He's gotta be figuring out
how to pay the overhead

00:17:09.410 --> 00:17:10.890
and how to grow the business,

00:17:10.890 --> 00:17:12.660
how to make it an
interesting place to work.

00:17:12.660 --> 00:17:15.840
It's a never, being a CEO/Founder,

00:17:15.840 --> 00:17:17.750
there's never enough hours in the day,

00:17:17.750 --> 00:17:21.223
and it never ends, never ends.

00:17:23.146 --> 00:17:25.230
We basically said to them that,

00:17:25.230 --> 00:17:27.380
they asked me if I'd take a look at it.

00:17:27.380 --> 00:17:28.620
We said yes, and we did.

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:30.470
Six months later, we had the account.

00:17:31.530 --> 00:17:34.160
- I wanna talk about hard
work, and get into it

00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:36.030
cause we're sitting here, two Goliaths,

00:17:36.030 --> 00:17:38.920
but really, your stories, and
how you relate your story,

00:17:38.920 --> 00:17:41.080
is about being the Davids.

00:17:41.080 --> 00:17:43.540
There's a paragraph in
your book, just came out,

00:17:43.540 --> 00:17:45.170
Who Is Michael Ovitz?,
that I really related to

00:17:45.170 --> 00:17:47.640
because it gets into the specifics.

00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:51.180
I'd love for you each to kind of continue

00:17:51.180 --> 00:17:53.033
this paragraph after I read it.

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:57.120
As agents, we didn't agree on anything.

00:17:57.120 --> 00:17:58.140
We were sellers.

00:17:58.140 --> 00:18:01.020
We sold our clients
our time and expertise,

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:03.150
and then we sold the buyers our clients.

00:18:03.150 --> 00:18:06.040
Our tenuous capital was
the hours in the day,

00:18:06.040 --> 00:18:08.740
less the few we slept,
and we spent that capital

00:18:08.740 --> 00:18:09.750
at frenzied pace.

00:18:09.750 --> 00:18:13.130
I'd drop everything to get
you the right cardiac surgeon,

00:18:13.130 --> 00:18:14.930
the right car, a place for your kid

00:18:14.930 --> 00:18:17.520
at the John Thomas Dye or
Harvard-Westlake Schools,

00:18:17.520 --> 00:18:18.710
whatever you needed.

00:18:18.710 --> 00:18:20.760
I was everyone's chief psychiatrist,

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:24.050
legal advisor, financial
fixer, cultural translator,

00:18:24.050 --> 00:18:25.373
and shoulder to cry on.

00:18:26.900 --> 00:18:28.650
- Would you take that first.

00:18:28.650 --> 00:18:30.005
- Period.

00:18:30.005 --> 00:18:30.838
- Cause you're doing it now.

00:18:30.838 --> 00:18:31.671
- [Elliot] Period.

00:18:31.671 --> 00:18:32.650
- You're doing it, I did it.

00:18:32.650 --> 00:18:34.611
- Yeah, I mean look, I think that,

00:18:34.611 --> 00:18:37.490
I think everybody here
could be empathetic.

00:18:37.490 --> 00:18:39.070
How many people here, by show of hands,

00:18:39.070 --> 00:18:41.330
are running their company,
or are the founder

00:18:41.330 --> 00:18:43.510
of their company, the
last line of defense.

00:18:43.510 --> 00:18:44.343
Just raise it.

00:18:45.449 --> 00:18:46.282
An enormous amount.

00:18:46.282 --> 00:18:49.400
Look, I think, thank god,
we're having very detailed,

00:18:49.400 --> 00:18:51.120
nuanced conversations in culture

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:53.240
about work-life balance,
about proper rest,

00:18:53.240 --> 00:18:55.540
about meditation, mental health.

00:18:55.540 --> 00:18:56.430
That makes me happy.

00:18:56.430 --> 00:19:00.440
I think a lot of times I love hard work

00:19:00.440 --> 00:19:02.780
as a competitive advantage, but I get

00:19:02.780 --> 00:19:06.100
thrown under the bus
of somebody who doesn't

00:19:06.100 --> 00:19:07.850
believe in what I do believe in,

00:19:07.850 --> 00:19:09.430
which is self-awareness.

00:19:09.430 --> 00:19:13.390
Like, if this is what you love,
and you love your process,

00:19:13.390 --> 00:19:17.020
I have no interest in judging
anybody's work-life balance,

00:19:17.020 --> 00:19:19.770
how they parent, or how
they live their lives

00:19:19.770 --> 00:19:21.570
cause I don't know the context.

00:19:21.570 --> 00:19:24.640
Who here would, I would
never want somebody

00:19:24.640 --> 00:19:26.940
to work so hard, or be stressed so much

00:19:26.940 --> 00:19:29.660
that they commit suicide,
or they don't feel well.

00:19:29.660 --> 00:19:31.650
That'd be ludicrous, that's ludicrous.

00:19:31.650 --> 00:19:33.790
At the same token, I have no interest

00:19:33.790 --> 00:19:36.410
in judging somebody who's so passionate

00:19:36.410 --> 00:19:38.500
about their work, or the things that they

00:19:38.500 --> 00:19:40.980
want to see in the world,
that during this period

00:19:40.980 --> 00:19:43.440
in their life, they're willing
to make that commitment

00:19:43.440 --> 00:19:44.440
to that product.

00:19:44.440 --> 00:19:46.930
Everybody who just
raised their hands knows,

00:19:46.930 --> 00:19:48.530
how many people of the people
that raised their hands

00:19:48.530 --> 00:19:49.780
also have a child?

00:19:49.780 --> 00:19:51.190
Raise your hand.

00:19:51.190 --> 00:19:53.380
Your business is the other child.

00:19:53.380 --> 00:19:55.140
You love it that much.

00:19:55.140 --> 00:19:56.480
It's just the truth.

00:19:56.480 --> 00:19:58.260
So, what do I think?

00:19:58.260 --> 00:20:01.020
I think when you're the
last line of defense

00:20:01.020 --> 00:20:03.960
within an organization, you're always on.

00:20:03.960 --> 00:20:05.610
It's just a cost of entry.

00:20:05.610 --> 00:20:08.540
I also think for a lot
of people in this room

00:20:08.540 --> 00:20:10.800
it's the only way they could breathe.

00:20:10.800 --> 00:20:13.640
On the flip side, the
thing I am concerned about

00:20:13.640 --> 00:20:15.650
is that I do think entrepreneurship,

00:20:15.650 --> 00:20:19.580
or being CEO, has been
glamorized over the last decade,

00:20:19.580 --> 00:20:22.660
and I do think that people are
not deploying self-awareness,

00:20:22.660 --> 00:20:25.380
and I do believe that a
high number of the hands

00:20:25.380 --> 00:20:28.050
that went up are actually more suited

00:20:28.050 --> 00:20:31.580
to be a number two or three,
but that the coolness,

00:20:31.580 --> 00:20:33.880
and the excitement, and
just the sheer amount

00:20:33.880 --> 00:20:37.670
of money in the system to
fund to be a number one

00:20:37.670 --> 00:20:40.240
is attractive to people.

00:20:40.240 --> 00:20:41.420
There's a part of me that loves it

00:20:41.420 --> 00:20:43.890
because I want everybody
to take a swing at it,

00:20:43.890 --> 00:20:45.970
but to me, it's about self-awareness.

00:20:45.970 --> 00:20:47.400
He loved it.

00:20:47.400 --> 00:20:49.289
He loved it.

00:20:49.289 --> 00:20:50.729
Do you know how much I love when somebody

00:20:50.729 --> 00:20:53.570
comes into my office and
says Karen's ruining me?

00:20:53.570 --> 00:20:56.770
Like, I'm the head of HR, like, I love it.

00:20:56.770 --> 00:20:59.210
But, three of my calls today were people

00:20:59.210 --> 00:21:01.530
who were not satisfied with their raise.

00:21:01.530 --> 00:21:03.250
I'm worried about the leak in the roof

00:21:03.250 --> 00:21:05.929
in our Chattanooga, Tennessee office.

00:21:05.929 --> 00:21:07.970
I love it.

00:21:07.970 --> 00:21:09.390
It's what I like to do.

00:21:09.390 --> 00:21:12.020
My brother hated managing people

00:21:12.020 --> 00:21:13.760
which is why he left VaynerMedia,

00:21:13.760 --> 00:21:15.660
and now is trying to be Jerry Maguire.

00:21:17.710 --> 00:21:20.030
So, I think self-awareness matters,

00:21:20.030 --> 00:21:23.630
but to me, when I hear that,
I'm like uh huh, uh huh.

00:21:23.630 --> 00:21:27.240
I wake up, we have a London office.

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:28.410
We're opening up Singapore.

00:21:28.410 --> 00:21:30.670
When I wake up, I look at my phone

00:21:30.670 --> 00:21:32.800
to make sure if there's
any fire I need to put out.

00:21:32.800 --> 00:21:34.930
I literally, you know, you
don't have to do it anymore

00:21:34.930 --> 00:21:36.580
with global entry, but for the last year

00:21:36.580 --> 00:21:38.120
that I was filling out the passport thing

00:21:38.120 --> 00:21:41.223
where it said occupation, I
literally wrote firefighter.

00:21:42.068 --> 00:21:43.190
(audience laughs)

00:21:43.190 --> 00:21:44.850
Because that's what we do.

00:21:44.850 --> 00:21:47.000
We're always on, ready to go,

00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:49.560
and when you're reverse
engineering a client,

00:21:49.560 --> 00:21:52.280
whether it's an organization or a human,

00:21:52.280 --> 00:21:54.607
you are there for them, and so if you need

00:21:54.607 --> 00:21:58.080
to be their financial advisor,
or cultural interpreter,

00:21:58.080 --> 00:21:59.760
or you gotta pick up their dog,

00:21:59.760 --> 00:22:01.290
these are things that
you're signing up for

00:22:01.290 --> 00:22:03.512
when you're in the
client services business.

00:22:03.512 --> 00:22:08.512
It fucking sucks, but
it's the cost of entry.

00:22:08.770 --> 00:22:13.547
- I think it is the most
complicated discussion

00:22:15.030 --> 00:22:16.853
that could possibly be had.

00:22:17.950 --> 00:22:22.010
I think that to be a CEO/Founder

00:22:22.010 --> 00:22:25.490
is probably one of the most
difficult jobs on the planet.

00:22:25.490 --> 00:22:30.420
I work with a lot of
founders up in the Bay Area.

00:22:30.420 --> 00:22:32.100
It's what I do now.

00:22:32.100 --> 00:22:34.680
I think their job is impossible.

00:22:34.680 --> 00:22:38.860
Every time you think you
have something under control,

00:22:38.860 --> 00:22:40.840
you have a leak in the
roof in Chattanooga.

00:22:40.840 --> 00:22:41.673
- That's right.

00:22:42.591 --> 00:22:44.360
- The reality is that one
of the pieces of advice,

00:22:44.360 --> 00:22:47.680
as simple and stupid as it
sounds, that I've given,

00:22:47.680 --> 00:22:51.220
is that when you think
things are going well,

00:22:51.220 --> 00:22:53.440
you're gonna walk around the corner

00:22:53.440 --> 00:22:55.220
and someone's gonna wack you in the shins

00:22:55.220 --> 00:22:58.930
with a two by four because they never

00:22:58.930 --> 00:23:01.170
ever go well.

00:23:01.170 --> 00:23:03.330
I had an incident with a startup up north

00:23:03.330 --> 00:23:05.180
that's done exceedingly well, taken off

00:23:05.180 --> 00:23:08.847
like a rocket ship on one of the advisors.

00:23:08.847 --> 00:23:12.410
I went in at the recommendation
of one of the investors

00:23:12.410 --> 00:23:14.100
to invest and advise.

00:23:14.100 --> 00:23:15.030
I did.

00:23:15.030 --> 00:23:16.560
I met with the two young founders,

00:23:16.560 --> 00:23:20.310
both brilliant, young engineers, mid 20's,

00:23:20.310 --> 00:23:23.260
and I said look, I'm gonna
give you a little bit

00:23:23.260 --> 00:23:25.730
of advice that I was asked to give you.

00:23:25.730 --> 00:23:26.710
You won't listen to it.

00:23:26.710 --> 00:23:27.820
You won't agree with it.

00:23:27.820 --> 00:23:29.940
When I walk outta here, you're gonna say

00:23:29.940 --> 00:23:31.793
that old guy is really an idiot.

00:23:33.089 --> 00:23:34.460
I'm not gonna blame you for thinking that,

00:23:34.460 --> 00:23:37.240
but within six months,
you're gonna call me

00:23:37.240 --> 00:23:38.590
cause you've got a problem.

00:23:39.851 --> 00:23:41.720
When I left, I looked back, and they

00:23:41.720 --> 00:23:45.300
were snickering a little, and
probably saying the things

00:23:45.300 --> 00:23:48.010
that I thought they would, and
that they probably should've.

00:23:48.010 --> 00:23:48.843
Well, I was wrong.

00:23:48.843 --> 00:23:51.250
It wasn't six months, it
was four months later.

00:23:51.250 --> 00:23:52.450
I was in Palo Alto.

00:23:52.450 --> 00:23:53.760
My phone rings.

00:23:53.760 --> 00:23:55.510
I was shocked it was one of the founders,

00:23:55.510 --> 00:23:58.233
shocked because he never
used the phone in his life.

00:23:59.220 --> 00:24:02.870
His idea of a communication
was a three word text.

00:24:02.870 --> 00:24:06.850
The words how are you feeling
never came out of his mouth.

00:24:06.850 --> 00:24:11.850
The first thing that I
heard was do you remember

00:24:11.900 --> 00:24:13.740
when you told us something would happen

00:24:13.740 --> 00:24:15.490
that we didn't expect.

00:24:15.490 --> 00:24:16.770
I said yes.

00:24:16.770 --> 00:24:18.080
He said, "Well, it happened."

00:24:18.080 --> 00:24:19.180
I said tell me.

00:24:19.180 --> 00:24:22.320
They told me that their
business had gotten shut down

00:24:22.320 --> 00:24:24.240
because they'd been hacked by some

00:24:24.240 --> 00:24:27.650
rogue Nigerian hackers,
which because I do work

00:24:27.650 --> 00:24:30.720
for a company called Palantir,
I actually advised them

00:24:30.720 --> 00:24:31.953
was going to happen.

00:24:33.170 --> 00:24:35.100
They were shut down, and they were

00:24:35.100 --> 00:24:36.280
in the e-commerce business.

00:24:36.280 --> 00:24:38.600
Now, for those of you that
are on the consumer side,

00:24:38.600 --> 00:24:41.770
picture yourself shutdown on a weekend,

00:24:41.770 --> 00:24:44.330
which is your highest traffic day.

00:24:44.330 --> 00:24:46.190
In any case, they had no relationships.

00:24:46.190 --> 00:24:47.380
They never reached out to anybody.

00:24:47.380 --> 00:24:50.640
They never cultivated any
relationships with anybody.

00:24:50.640 --> 00:24:53.520
I was fortunate enough
to had just had dinner

00:24:53.520 --> 00:24:56.600
with the man who ran
PayPal, who just happened

00:24:56.600 --> 00:25:00.373
to purchase the company
that shut them down.

00:25:01.400 --> 00:25:03.920
I called that person, congratulated him

00:25:03.920 --> 00:25:06.100
on his sale, said by the way,

00:25:06.100 --> 00:25:07.220
can you do me a favor.

00:25:07.220 --> 00:25:08.450
Of course, he did.

00:25:08.450 --> 00:25:10.070
They were back up.

00:25:10.070 --> 00:25:13.892
That lesson to them did not go unheeded.

00:25:13.892 --> 00:25:16.430
That lesson of trying
to build relationships,

00:25:16.430 --> 00:25:19.120
build a network, deal with people,

00:25:19.120 --> 00:25:21.190
and try to make sure that you put in

00:25:21.190 --> 00:25:24.110
a certain amount of
time in that direction.

00:25:24.110 --> 00:25:27.670
Going back to the question at hand,

00:25:27.670 --> 00:25:30.010
it's very hard when you're a workaholic

00:25:30.010 --> 00:25:34.010
like Gary or myself, and
you enjoy the action.

00:25:34.010 --> 00:25:36.880
You just enjoy the day to day fight.

00:25:36.880 --> 00:25:40.640
You enjoy every part of it.

00:25:40.640 --> 00:25:42.670
One has to remember as a founder,

00:25:42.670 --> 00:25:46.240
you can't expect everyone in your staff

00:25:46.240 --> 00:25:49.310
to have the same persona that you do.

00:25:49.310 --> 00:25:51.193
- I actually think you can't.

00:25:52.340 --> 00:25:55.260
I'm also an investor and
advisor in a lot of businesses,

00:25:55.260 --> 00:25:57.180
and obviously get a ton of DMs and emails

00:25:57.180 --> 00:25:58.510
from entrepreneurs.

00:25:58.510 --> 00:26:01.580
The audacity for you to want somebody

00:26:01.580 --> 00:26:04.490
who's not compensated in
the same way that you are,

00:26:04.490 --> 00:26:06.860
to work the same way that you do

00:26:06.860 --> 00:26:09.340
is one of the great shocking things

00:26:09.340 --> 00:26:10.250
that I watch about.

00:26:10.250 --> 00:26:12.510
Gary, I want them to work as hard as I do.

00:26:12.510 --> 00:26:15.112
I'm like give them 50% of the business.

00:26:15.112 --> 00:26:17.000
(audience laughs)

00:26:17.000 --> 00:26:18.220
It's ludicrous.

00:26:18.220 --> 00:26:22.450
I have never, I have
actually, I've been fascinated

00:26:22.450 --> 00:26:23.767
by that.

00:26:23.767 --> 00:26:25.430
I've never had the expectation of any,

00:26:25.430 --> 00:26:27.770
I feel like the almost thousand people

00:26:27.770 --> 00:26:30.309
that I work for, which is the other thing

00:26:30.309 --> 00:26:31.840
that you learn very quickly
if you're a good leader

00:26:31.840 --> 00:26:35.500
or entrepreneur, or a
CEO, is you work for them.

00:26:35.500 --> 00:26:37.120
They don't work for you, which is why

00:26:37.120 --> 00:26:39.310
so many through your careers struggled

00:26:39.310 --> 00:26:41.140
with the transition from being a doer

00:26:41.140 --> 00:26:42.800
to being a manager, cause you thought

00:26:42.800 --> 00:26:45.430
that was the graduation, and
it's actually the reverse.

00:26:45.430 --> 00:26:47.950
That's why people struggle with that.

00:26:47.950 --> 00:26:50.840
It's crazy to me that
people have that audacity.

00:26:50.840 --> 00:26:52.670
- I think you raised a really good point

00:26:52.670 --> 00:26:56.130
about the fact that you
work for all your employees.

00:26:56.130 --> 00:26:58.380
I do think that there
is a way, and you do it.

00:26:58.380 --> 00:27:00.800
I watched you do it when
I'm up at your office.

00:27:00.800 --> 00:27:04.940
I did the same thing, which is
you can only lead by example.

00:27:04.940 --> 00:27:06.180
It's the only thing you can do.

00:27:06.180 --> 00:27:09.040
- Yeah, but, me going at it hard, for me,

00:27:09.040 --> 00:27:11.640
I think I wanna know the context.

00:27:11.640 --> 00:27:13.430
Everybody's driven by different things.

00:27:13.430 --> 00:27:14.990
Some people are driven by titles.

00:27:14.990 --> 00:27:16.780
Some people are driven by finances.

00:27:16.780 --> 00:27:18.720
Some people are driven
by work-life balance.

00:27:18.720 --> 00:27:20.800
For me, it's always being on.

00:27:20.800 --> 00:27:23.010
Today, Sarah wants money.

00:27:23.010 --> 00:27:24.940
Next week, she falls in love,

00:27:24.940 --> 00:27:27.060
and she wants to spend
more time with that person.

00:27:27.060 --> 00:27:28.710
So, she needs a little time off.

00:27:28.710 --> 00:27:31.180
In four years, somebody
that used to work for her

00:27:31.180 --> 00:27:33.350
now has a bigger title than her.

00:27:33.350 --> 00:27:34.780
You're just always on.

00:27:34.780 --> 00:27:37.150
I feel like not only do I
have to reverse engineer

00:27:37.150 --> 00:27:39.670
what they care about, you know, period,

00:27:39.670 --> 00:27:42.810
I have to be that way in
perpetuity while they're with me

00:27:42.810 --> 00:27:44.250
because they're gonna have to evolve.

00:27:44.250 --> 00:27:46.520
They will evolve, and I have to create

00:27:46.520 --> 00:27:48.950
the foundation for them
to be able to navigate.

00:27:48.950 --> 00:27:50.130
Otherwise, I don't bring value,

00:27:50.130 --> 00:27:52.110
and they have to find an alternative.

00:27:52.110 --> 00:27:55.410
- That goes to the point that
it is incredibly complex.

00:27:55.410 --> 00:27:56.687
- Massive.

00:27:56.687 --> 00:27:58.228
- Look at the roadmap you just drew.

00:27:58.228 --> 00:28:01.912
Those that lead have to
make certain sacrifices

00:28:01.912 --> 00:28:04.190
to those who don't.

00:28:04.190 --> 00:28:05.520
- You know what, that brings
up an interesting point.

00:28:05.520 --> 00:28:07.620
The other thing that I
think is super interesting

00:28:07.620 --> 00:28:10.110
is that it's in perpetuity.

00:28:10.110 --> 00:28:12.470
I always laughed that people boo or cheer

00:28:12.470 --> 00:28:14.420
in the second quarter.

00:28:14.420 --> 00:28:15.690
Let me promise you one thing.

00:28:15.690 --> 00:28:18.190
How many people, again,
running their own company?

00:28:19.270 --> 00:28:20.990
It's hard, right?

00:28:20.990 --> 00:28:24.330
Guess what, this is the
easiest time to do it.

00:28:24.330 --> 00:28:26.250
The amount of fucking
money that's in the system,

00:28:26.250 --> 00:28:28.070
and the amount of you that
have bullshit businesses

00:28:28.070 --> 00:28:30.810
that actually aren't
businesses, but are funded,

00:28:30.810 --> 00:28:33.970
being serious, this is
something we have to talk about.

00:28:33.970 --> 00:28:36.500
If you think it's hard
now, one of the things

00:28:36.500 --> 00:28:38.580
that I'm most proud of
is that from the day

00:28:38.580 --> 00:28:40.060
I started running my dad's business,

00:28:40.060 --> 00:28:42.060
20 plus years ago to this moment,

00:28:42.060 --> 00:28:44.040
I've been running the
company, and I've never

00:28:44.040 --> 00:28:45.950
had a credit line that
I took for Wine Library.

00:28:45.950 --> 00:28:47.240
I never raised capital.

00:28:47.240 --> 00:28:51.020
I've paid my bills weekly in perpetuity.

00:28:51.020 --> 00:28:53.700
I have never lost $6 million this year,

00:28:53.700 --> 00:28:56.720
and did all my behavior
to get enough CAC and LTV

00:28:56.720 --> 00:28:58.610
to get the next fundraising round.

00:28:58.610 --> 00:28:59.940
You do understand that everybody's

00:28:59.940 --> 00:29:02.472
about to get smoked the fuck out, right?

00:29:02.472 --> 00:29:03.305
(audience laughs)

00:29:03.305 --> 00:29:04.960
Everybody's so over-leveraged.

00:29:04.960 --> 00:29:06.320
They don't have businesses.

00:29:06.320 --> 00:29:10.573
They have VC arbitraged machines
for the next fundraising.

00:29:11.870 --> 00:29:13.700
I'll see you all working
at Bank of America

00:29:13.700 --> 00:29:15.092
in a couple years.

00:29:15.092 --> 00:29:18.569
(audience applauds)

00:29:18.569 --> 00:29:19.402
It's 100% true.

00:29:19.402 --> 00:29:20.410
Let me say this.

00:29:20.410 --> 00:29:22.110
I'm not sitting up here like I'm cool

00:29:22.110 --> 00:29:23.280
and I'm razzing you.

00:29:23.280 --> 00:29:24.980
I'm hoping that that hits you in a way

00:29:24.980 --> 00:29:26.530
that says fuck, maybe I should actually

00:29:26.530 --> 00:29:29.910
build a business, not
something that appeases

00:29:29.910 --> 00:29:32.680
the VCs who have no vested
interest in my business

00:29:32.680 --> 00:29:34.500
to get the next fundraising round.

00:29:34.500 --> 00:29:36.550
That's how you'll navigate the shit.

00:29:36.550 --> 00:29:38.231
You'll be practical.

00:29:38.231 --> 00:29:41.390
(audience applauds)

00:29:41.390 --> 00:29:43.020
I like how many people clapped

00:29:43.020 --> 00:29:45.319
verses how many people did not.

00:29:45.319 --> 00:29:46.850
(audience laughs)

00:29:46.850 --> 00:29:48.400
- I might hope that maybe somebody

00:29:48.400 --> 00:29:49.883
might make a pivot just.

00:29:51.527 --> 00:29:52.360
- Michael, look, I think one of the things

00:29:52.360 --> 00:29:53.570
I'm most fascinated by right now

00:29:53.570 --> 00:29:55.890
is there's so much money in the system.

00:29:55.890 --> 00:29:56.723
- Right.

00:29:56.723 --> 00:29:58.230
- And if you follow where
the money comes from,

00:29:58.230 --> 00:30:00.770
that's an interesting thing
to debate in the first place.

00:30:00.770 --> 00:30:03.460
But, let's leave that for another day.

00:30:03.460 --> 00:30:05.780
I am fascinated by people thinking

00:30:05.780 --> 00:30:09.860
that not making more profit than expenses

00:30:09.860 --> 00:30:12.620
is not something you need
to think about a lot more.

00:30:12.620 --> 00:30:14.960
Like, I'm just watching
people spend all their time

00:30:14.960 --> 00:30:18.470
on like influencer
marketing, and CAC, and MNA,

00:30:18.470 --> 00:30:21.380
and like, when the money dries out,

00:30:21.380 --> 00:30:23.510
there's a lot of young
people in this system.

00:30:23.510 --> 00:30:26.540
You didn't live through
2007 as an operator.

00:30:26.540 --> 00:30:28.460
You were in college having fun.

00:30:28.460 --> 00:30:30.720
You didn't live through 2001.

00:30:30.720 --> 00:30:33.500
We have never been this over leveraged.

00:30:33.500 --> 00:30:35.540
We have never been this over leveraged.

00:30:35.540 --> 00:30:37.430
When the dominoes fall, and there's no

00:30:37.430 --> 00:30:40.280
next fundraising round, or
when you're an influencer,

00:30:40.280 --> 00:30:42.160
and nobody's got $8,000 for you

00:30:42.160 --> 00:30:44.560
to take a picture with
a fucking toothpaste

00:30:44.560 --> 00:30:49.560
in your hand, it's not gonna be fun

00:30:49.670 --> 00:30:51.330
to go from traveling the world

00:30:51.330 --> 00:30:54.150
as an influencer to no
money in the system,

00:30:54.150 --> 00:30:55.700
and you have to go get a job.

00:30:55.700 --> 00:30:57.100
I want people to be prepared,

00:30:57.100 --> 00:30:59.300
and everyone's living
in fucking La-La Land.

00:31:00.930 --> 00:31:02.730
We've been living in
La-La Land for a decade,

00:31:02.730 --> 00:31:04.543
which makes me nervous as fuck.

00:31:05.840 --> 00:31:07.042
Thank you.

00:31:07.042 --> 00:31:10.042
(audience applauds)

00:31:15.330 --> 00:31:17.480
- [Elliot] I wanna ask a
question about deal making.

00:31:17.480 --> 00:31:19.104
Looking back on it--

00:31:19.104 --> 00:31:19.937
(audience laughs)

00:31:19.937 --> 00:31:22.300
- The segue is always so hard.

00:31:22.300 --> 00:31:24.713
- To continue, it's just
going right on this.

00:31:24.713 --> 00:31:25.546
I just wanna ask.

00:31:25.546 --> 00:31:27.706
- After what he said, I'm gonna retire.

00:31:27.706 --> 00:31:29.020
(audience laughs)

00:31:29.020 --> 00:31:30.120
Cause there's no hope.

00:31:31.346 --> 00:31:32.350
There's no hope.

00:31:32.350 --> 00:31:33.970
- Actually, that was super hopeful.

00:31:33.970 --> 00:31:35.080
- I guess it was.

00:31:35.080 --> 00:31:36.430
- No, it really was.

00:31:36.430 --> 00:31:38.620
The no hope is to live in the delusion

00:31:38.620 --> 00:31:40.500
that if you run a business, losing money

00:31:40.500 --> 00:31:41.670
is a viable option.

00:31:41.670 --> 00:31:43.700
- Well, but I agree with that.

00:31:43.700 --> 00:31:45.220
- Well, guess what,
Michael, this is fucking

00:31:45.220 --> 00:31:48.350
an ecosystem right now that
is built on losing money.

00:31:48.350 --> 00:31:50.657
- And I happen to believe that

00:31:51.629 --> 00:31:52.462
that is something that's gonna change.

00:31:52.462 --> 00:31:55.270
I think that the people that
are handing out the money

00:31:55.270 --> 00:31:56.510
while they still have it--

00:31:56.510 --> 00:31:58.090
- Correct.

00:31:58.090 --> 00:31:59.088
- Are starting to think through,

00:31:59.088 --> 00:32:00.040
and probably listening to your podcast.

00:32:00.040 --> 00:32:03.550
- Cause everybody,
because what Michael knows

00:32:03.550 --> 00:32:05.300
way better than I do,
but I've got at least

00:32:05.300 --> 00:32:08.453
a gray hair or two to know,
this is what always happens.

00:32:09.780 --> 00:32:11.360
We're not guessing up here.

00:32:11.360 --> 00:32:12.750
This is cycles.

00:32:12.750 --> 00:32:14.340
When you haven't been through one,

00:32:14.340 --> 00:32:16.333
you don't know the context.

00:32:18.169 --> 00:32:23.120
- When we started CAA, we
started with a very small

00:32:23.120 --> 00:32:23.953
amount of debt.

00:32:23.953 --> 00:32:26.360
We borrowed $100,000.

00:32:26.360 --> 00:32:29.920
We paid it off four
months after we started.

00:32:29.920 --> 00:32:33.160
We had zero debt for the entire time

00:32:33.160 --> 00:32:34.420
I was at the company.

00:32:34.420 --> 00:32:39.420
The reason was exactly, and again,

00:32:39.827 --> 00:32:42.100
this is 40 years ago, and the reason

00:32:42.100 --> 00:32:44.780
was exactly what Gary is saying.

00:32:44.780 --> 00:32:46.886
40 years ago, it was the same problem.

00:32:46.886 --> 00:32:49.485
There was too much cash in the system.

00:32:49.485 --> 00:32:51.980
There were a lot of
businesses starting out

00:32:51.980 --> 00:32:53.840
that shouldn't have started out.

00:32:53.840 --> 00:32:55.530
There were a lot of
businesses starting out

00:32:55.530 --> 00:32:58.190
that should've started out, but they were

00:32:58.190 --> 00:33:00.130
over funded and worried.

00:33:00.130 --> 00:33:02.270
They weren't worried
about making a profit.

00:33:02.270 --> 00:33:05.140
I will say, and I can
speak for Gary on this

00:33:05.140 --> 00:33:08.010
because I admire how he runs his business,

00:33:08.010 --> 00:33:10.080
when I ran CAA, there was only one thing

00:33:10.080 --> 00:33:13.100
that was important, and that
was secondary, of course,

00:33:13.100 --> 00:33:16.350
to our people, which was
the most important thing,

00:33:16.350 --> 00:33:19.530
most important, and
we've commented on that.

00:33:19.530 --> 00:33:21.450
But, it was to turn a profit, and not

00:33:21.450 --> 00:33:23.660
to be in anybody's back pocket,

00:33:23.660 --> 00:33:26.620
and not have anybody being in a position

00:33:26.620 --> 00:33:28.400
to tell us what to do.

00:33:28.400 --> 00:33:32.220
We were gonna rise or
fall on our own deeds,

00:33:32.220 --> 00:33:34.950
our own brains, our own will power,

00:33:34.950 --> 00:33:36.870
and what we could do.

00:33:36.870 --> 00:33:40.740
So, it's a, to me, past is prologue.

00:33:40.740 --> 00:33:44.660
When Gary mentioned cycles,
it's the same way to say it.

00:33:44.660 --> 00:33:46.970
History always repeats itself.

00:33:46.970 --> 00:33:49.310
We're seeing that now in the stock market.

00:33:49.310 --> 00:33:50.360
We're going to see it.

00:33:50.360 --> 00:33:52.410
We're seeing it in world affairs,

00:33:52.410 --> 00:33:57.130
and we're seeing it up
north in the startup area.

00:33:57.130 --> 00:34:00.220
We're seeing it with black
swan companies as well.

00:34:00.220 --> 00:34:02.530
I mean, let's not forget that as good

00:34:02.530 --> 00:34:04.340
as Apple's report was yesterday,

00:34:04.340 --> 00:34:06.737
the stock was still,
when I last looked down,

00:34:06.737 --> 00:34:10.130
$15 today before I turned my phone off.

00:34:10.130 --> 00:34:14.360
So, we're in a very
cyclic world right now.

00:34:14.360 --> 00:34:16.720
- I wanna ask one more question
to expand right on this.

00:34:16.720 --> 00:34:18.830
I think it's a fun way to end.

00:34:18.830 --> 00:34:19.930
You guys have been fantastic.

00:34:19.930 --> 00:34:23.731
I wanna ask you, Gary, you say
it's your number one thesis,

00:34:23.731 --> 00:34:25.850
so much of what we can learn about how

00:34:25.850 --> 00:34:27.940
the world works is based on this.

00:34:27.940 --> 00:34:29.140
Hip hop rules the world.

00:34:30.150 --> 00:34:32.390
- So, I think that if you look,

00:34:32.390 --> 00:34:33.950
Michael said something super important,

00:34:33.950 --> 00:34:36.410
which was that history repeats itself.

00:34:36.410 --> 00:34:39.180
I think if you really study the impact

00:34:39.180 --> 00:34:42.040
that rock and roll had on our society,

00:34:42.040 --> 00:34:44.460
all the way through to
legislation, governments,

00:34:44.460 --> 00:34:46.460
the way we feel about many issues,

00:34:46.460 --> 00:34:48.980
I would argue that for many of us

00:34:48.980 --> 00:34:52.530
who are between the ages of 35 to 55,

00:34:52.530 --> 00:34:54.187
who, if you just look at history,

00:34:54.187 --> 00:34:56.170
had a very big impact on where the world

00:34:56.170 --> 00:34:59.110
goes and how, that many of us grew up

00:34:59.110 --> 00:35:02.260
in a hip hop environment,
and with that seed,

00:35:02.260 --> 00:35:04.310
where I look at, it's crazy for me

00:35:04.310 --> 00:35:07.570
to see Cypress Hill is The Who.

00:35:07.570 --> 00:35:11.390
Run DMC and Beastie
Boys are Rolling Stones.

00:35:11.390 --> 00:35:14.060
It's amazing to see history repeat itself.

00:35:14.060 --> 00:35:18.050
So, look, I study where the pebble is

